05a-0b0c-5f15-b9c1-1d00ad746eb6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533970 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533970 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533970 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533970 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533970 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533970 #edb/ref 1089 #edb/ref 17592186533971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533970 #edb/ref 1090 #uuid "79110b9e-fd73-5efb-8ce0-acef8691262c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533971 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186534100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533971 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186534101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533971 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186534114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533971 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186534125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533971 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186534135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533971 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186534137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533971 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186534138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533971 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186534150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533971 #edb/ref 1085 "Let $f(x)$ be a continuously differentiable function defined for all real $x.$ Values for $f$ and some of its derivatives at particular points are shown below.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n| **$x$** | **$f(x)$** | **${f}^{′}(x)$** | **${f}^{″}(x)$** | **${f}^{‴}(x)$** | **${f}^{(4)}(x)$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $9$ | $-1$ | $-5$ | $-4$ | $-2$ |\n| $3$ | $6$ | $-5$ | $-6$ | $-6$ | $-3$ |\n| $4$ | $-5$ | $-7$ | $∗$ | $-13$ | $-6$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nIn the interval $x\\in [2,4],$ the function $f$ and its derivatives are all decreasing." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533971 #edb/ref 1086 #uuid "8fc7ff53-1fcb-5909-be7f-1625560888f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533971 #edb/ref 1087 #edb/ref 17592186534138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533982 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533982 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127237" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533982 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533982 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ found in part 1 to estimate the value of $f(4.1).$\n\n$f(4.1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533982 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "18880848-70e4-5a06-adb0-54fd08f27bb7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186533982 #edb/ref 1074 "Our tangent line approximation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =-7x+23.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(4.1)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=4.1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4.1) & ≈L(4.1) \\\\  & =-7(4.1)+23 \\\\  & =-5.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534005 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534005 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127236" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534005 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534005 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the function $L(x),$ the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=4.$\n\n$L(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534005 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "51c7f8d6-81b7-561f-9aaa-77f7befa4a88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534005 #edb/ref 1074 "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(4)+{f}^{′}(4)(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have $f(4)=-5$ and ${f}^{′}(4)=-7,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =-5-7(x-4) \\\\  & =-5-7x+28 \\\\  & =-7x+23.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534044 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534044 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127243" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534044 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534044 #edb/ref 1071 "According to the Lagrange error bound, the third-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=4$ approximates $f(3.9)$ with an error that is less than{{field-1}}\n(Do not round your answer, write it as a fraction)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534044 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a8664628-f85e-5ad6-803e-867eedd4f296" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534044 #edb/ref 1074 "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{3}$ obtained when using the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{4!}|x-4{|}^{4},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=4$ and $x=3.91.$ Plugging $x=3.9$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{4!}|3.9-4{|}^{4} \\\\ |{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{24}|-0.1{|}^{4} \\\\ |{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{24}(0.1)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to find the maximum of $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ on the interval $[3.9,4].$\n\nSince $f$ and all its derivatives are decreasing on the interval $[2,4],$ and since ${f}^{(4)}(x)$ is negative in this interval, we know that $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ is an increasing function on the interval $[3.9,4]$ and its value is greatest at $x=4.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(4)}(4)|=|-6|=6,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{6}{24}(0.1)^{4}=\\frac{1}{40\\,000}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534048 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534048 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127242" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534048 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534048 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the third-degree Taylor polynomial approximation of $f(x)$ about $x=4$ to estimate the value of $f(3.9).$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n$f(3.9)≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534048 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "adda2b34-6b6b-5cc9-aece-62e12d1c6b0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534048 #edb/ref 1074 "The third-degree Taylor polynomial approximation is\n\n$$\n{P}_{3}(x)=-5-7(x-4)-5(x-4)^{2}-\\frac{13}{6}(x-4)^{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(3.9)$ by evaluating the third-degree Taylor polynomial approximation at $x=3.9.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3.9) & ≈{P}_{3}(3.9) \\\\  & =-5-7(3.9-4)-5(3.9-4)^{2}-\\frac{13}{6}(3.9-4)^{3} \\\\  & =-5-7(-0.1)-5(-0.1)^{2}-\\frac{13}{6}(-0.1)^{3} \\\\  & ≈-4.348.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534053 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534053 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127239" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534053 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534053 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the second-degree Taylor polynomial approximation of $f(x)$ found in part 3 to estimate the value of $f(3.2).$\n\n$f(3.2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534053 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b64844a1-1d87-5ee1-b6d0-65e60fd57ed8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534053 #edb/ref 1074 "Our second-degree Taylor polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =6-5(x-3)-3(x-3)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(3.2)$ by evaluating the second-degree Taylor polynomial approximation at $x=3.2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3.2) & ≈{P}_{2}(3.2) \\\\  & =6-5(3.2-3)-3(3.2-3)^{2} \\\\  & =6-5(0.2)-3(0.2)^{2} \\\\  & =4.88.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534063 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534063 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127241" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534063 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534063 #edb/ref 1071 "Write the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=4.$\n\n${P}_{3}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534063 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c4371116-bdf9-59eb-a9df-f28fb131982a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534063 #edb/ref 1074 "The third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =f(4)+{f}^{′}(4)(x-4)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(4)}{2!}(x-4)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(4)}{3!}(x-4)^{3} \\\\  & =f(4)+{f}^{′}(4)(x-4)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(4)}{2}(x-4)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(4)}{6}(x-4)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have the following set of values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4)=-5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(4)=-7 \\\\ {f}^{″}(4)=-10 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(4)=-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values into our Taylor Polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =-5-7(x-4)+\\frac{(-10)}{2}(x-4)^{2}+\\frac{(-13)}{6}(x-4)^{3} \\\\  & =-5-7(x-4)-5(x-4)^{2}-\\frac{13}{6}(x-4)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534088 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534088 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127238" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534088 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534088 #edb/ref 1071 "Write the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=3.$\n\n${P}_{2}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534088 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f1458ba0-7c76-5ea6-80a7-b0ed7100bb61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534088 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=3,$ we plug $a=3$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(3)+{f}^{′}(3)(x-3)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(3)(x-3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $f(3)=6,$ ${f}^{′}(3)=-5,$ and ${f}^{″}(3)=-6$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =6-5(x-3)+\\frac{1}{2}(-6)(x-3)^{2} \\\\  & =6-5(x-3)-3(x-3)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534091 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186533819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534091 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127240" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534091 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534091 #edb/ref 1071 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial approximation of $f(x)$ about $x=4$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-5-7(x-4)-5(x-4)^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe value of ${f}^{″}(4)=$ {{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534091 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f57da71f-a9a6-51ae-a7bf-060b8a16f967" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534091 #edb/ref 1074 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=4$ is given by\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(4)+{f}^{′}(4)(x-4)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(4)(x-4)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe're given an expansion about the point $x=4,$ so the coefficient of the second-degree term is $\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(4).$ In the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the second-degree term is $-5,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(4)=-5\\,⟹\\,{f}^{″}(4)=-10.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534100 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "03306394-e329-5b33-84ba-17768bef84ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534100 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186534044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534101 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186534137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534101 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0580bb03-6661-59c7-abd2-59ce43dffb45" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534101 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186534063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534114 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186534101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534114 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2b687f33-c2d5-5f9c-a584-2f9511726de7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534114 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186534091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534125 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186534114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534125 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "57000baf-4e57-5c3f-9071-c200d2ee5ca5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534125 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186534053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534135 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186534125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534135 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7e50a860-e18c-5d32-aa46-f0428b0e5f3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534135 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186534088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534137 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186534100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534137 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8ca132fc-de6e-59cd-aec9-a613eb401d7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534137 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/